WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.091
Seven towns.

2
00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:05.615
A company wants to connect

3
00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.950
them all with cable

4
00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.420
so that any town can reach any other.

5
00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.180
Each possible link has a price

6
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.180
depending on the distance and the terrain.

7
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.940
Eleven links are possible.

8
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.410
Building them all would be wasteful.

9
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.510
Which links should we build?

10
00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.130
Enough to connect everyone

11
00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.960
at the smallest total cost.

12
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.310
This is the last video of the chapter

13
00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.180
minimum spanning trees.

14
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.734
We'll see what we are looking for

15
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.169
Kruskal's algorithm and the

16
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.460
data structure it needs

17
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.010
and Prim's algorithm.

18
00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:38.823
What do we want?

19
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.870
A set of edges that connects

20
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.280
all the vertices

21
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.470
and has the least total weight.

22
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.648
If it has a cycle

23
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.614
we can drop one edge of the cycle

24
00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.290
and still be connected, and cheaper.

25
00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:54.460
So the best answer has no cycle.

26
00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.500
A connected graph with no cycle is a tree.

27
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.200
With seven vertices

28
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.277
a tree has exactly six edges.

29
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.528
A tree that spans every vertex

30
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.139
is a spanning tree

31
00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.149
and the cheapest one is the

32
00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.090
minimum spanning tree.

33
00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.512
Here is the idea that makes

34
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.340
a greedy algorithm safe.

35
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.230
Split the vertices into any two groups.

36
00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.496
Look at all edges with one

37
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:21.764
end in each group.

38
00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.942
The cheapest of them belongs to

39
00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.330
the minimum spanning tree.

40
00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.340
This is the cut property.

41
00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.540
Take the group A, D, F, against the rest.

42
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.200
The edges between them cost six, eleven

43
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.580
thirteen, nine and twelve.

44
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.640
The cheapest is A to B, at six

45
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.290
and indeed it is in the tree.

46
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:44.710
Why?

47
00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.030
If the tree did not use it

48
00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.659
adding it would create a cycle

49
00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:50.672
and that cycle must cross

50
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.470
the cut a second time

51
00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.770
through a more expensive edge.

52
00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:56.880
Swap them, and the tree gets cheaper.

53
00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.070
Kruskal's algorithm.

54
00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.950
Sort all edges by weight

55
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.700
and go through them from the cheapest.

56
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.609
Add an edge if it joins two vertices

57
00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.500
that are not yet connected.

58
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.060
Skip it if they already are

59
00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.770
because it would close a cycle.

60
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.410
Our edges.

61
00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:16.070
A to D, three: accept.

62
00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.150
C to E, four: accept.

63
00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.090
D to F, five.

64
00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.410
A to B, six.

65
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:24.970
B to E, seven.

66
00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:26.200
All accepted.

67
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.640
B to C, eight

68
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.700
B and C are already connected, through E.

69
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:32.900
Reject.

70
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.380
E to F, nine: already connected.

71
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.390
Reject.

72
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.910
E to G, ten: G is new.

73
00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:41.923
Accept.

74
00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:43.704
The remaining three edges

75
00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.490
would all close cycles.

76
00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.540
Reject.

77
00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.420
Six edges, total weight thirty-five.

78
00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.960
We built the whole network for thirty-five

79
00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.770
instead of eighty-eight.

80
00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.560
Kruskal keeps asking one question

81
00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.803
are these two vertices already connected?

82
00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:01.921
Re-searching the graph each

83
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.150
time would be slow.

84
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.670
A data structure answers it: union-find

85
00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.888
also called disjoint sets.

86
00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.026
Each group of connected vertices

87
00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.470
is a little tree

88
00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:15.340
and the root is the group's name.

89
00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.990
Union joins two groups by hanging

90
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.618
one root under the other.

91
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.341
Always hang the smaller tree

92
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.580
under the bigger one

93
00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.450
and the trees stay shallow.

94
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.224
Find walks up from a vertex to its root.

95
00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.214
Two vertices are in the same group

96
00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:31.880
when their roots are equal.

97
00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.614
How shallow?

98
00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.686
Take a thousand and twenty-four

99
00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:36.730
separate vertices

100
00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.150
and merge them in the worst way: pairs

101
00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.540
then pairs of pairs, and so on.

102
00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.992
The deepest vertex ends up only

103
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.780
ten steps from its root.

104
00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.380
Ten is the logarithm, base two

105
00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.850
of a thousand and twenty-four.

106
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.190
There is a second trick.

107
00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.576
When find walks up a long path

108
00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.863
it can point every vertex on that

109
00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.790
path straight at the root.

110
00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.760
This is path compression.

111
00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.478
A chain of ten vertices has its bottom

112
00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.900
vertex nine steps from the root.

113
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.800
After one find

114
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.430
no vertex is more than one step away.

115
00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.780
Prim's algorithm grows a single tree

116
00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.420
starting from any vertex.

117
00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:17.770
Say, A.

118
00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.370
At each step

119
00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.470
look at all edges leaving the tree

120
00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.030
and add the cheapest one.

121
00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:25.040
A priority queue finds it.

122
00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.980
From A: A to D, three.

123
00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:30.460
Then D to F, five.

124
00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.840
Then A to B, six.

125
00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.140
Then B to E, seven.

126
00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.500
Then E to C, four.

127
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.990
Then E to G, ten.

128
00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.590
The same total, thirty-five.

129
00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:43.565
The same tree.

130
00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:45.610
The two algorithms take different

131
00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.820
paths to the same answer.

132
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.717
Pause and predict.

133
00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:51.389
The cable network we built

134
00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:52.822
is cheapest overall.

135
00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.768
Does it also give the cheapest

136
00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.560
route from A to G?

137
00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:58.200
Inside the tree

138
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.960
the only route from A to G is A, B, E, G.

139
00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.460
Six, plus seven, plus ten.

140
00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:06.630
Twenty-three.

141
00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.510
But the cheapest route in the whole graph

142
00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.900
from last time, is A, D, F, G, at twenty.

143
00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.081
And the tree of cheapest routes

144
00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.100
from A costs forty-one

145
00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.380
against thirty-five for the spanning tree.

146
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.650
Different questions, different trees.

147
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.412
The cost of Kruskal is dominated

148
00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:28.920
by sorting the edges: order E log E.

149
00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:32.060
Since E is at most V squared

150
00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.300
that is order E log V.

151
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.750
The union-find steps are almost constant.

152
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.940
Prim with a heap also costs order E log V.

153
00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.420
For a graph with lots of edges

154
00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:46.720
Prim with a plain array can be better.

155
00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.410
For most sparse graphs, either is fine.

156
00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:50.927
To sum up.

157
00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.639
A minimum spanning tree connects everything

158
00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.148
with the least total weight

159
00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.316
and by the cut property a

160
00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.350
greedy choice is safe.

161
00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.037
Kruskal sorts the edges and uses

162
00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.330
union-find to skip cycles.

163
00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.780
Prim grows one tree with a priority queue.

164
00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.200
And that is the graph chapter

165
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.820
how to store a graph, how to search it

166
00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:14.340
how to find cheapest routes

167
00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.810
and how to connect everything cheaply.

168
00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:18.977
It is also the end of our

169
00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.950
tour of data structures.

170
00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:23.910
Arrays, lists, stacks, trees

171
00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:26.410
hash tables and heaps are the parts.

172
00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:28.840
Graphs are what you can build out of them.

173
00:06:29.467 --> 00:06:31.657
Let's write union-find

174
00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:34.107
Kruskal and Prim in Java.

175
00:06:34.687 --> 00:06:37.137
Two maps: each element's parent

176
00:06:37.137 --> 00:06:39.297
and the size of each set.

177
00:06:39.297 --> 00:06:41.807
A root is its own parent.

178
00:06:42.157 --> 00:06:44.112
Find walks up to the root.

179
00:06:44.187 --> 00:06:46.758
A second loop walks the same path and

180
00:06:46.827 --> 00:06:48.907
points each element at the root

181
00:06:48.907 --> 00:06:50.317
path compression.

182
00:06:50.667 --> 00:06:52.717
Because the class is generic

183
00:06:52.717 --> 00:06:54.697
we compare with equals

184
00:06:54.697 --> 00:06:56.467
not with the double equals sign.

185
00:06:57.047 --> 00:06:58.817
Union finds both roots.

186
00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:02.007
If they are equal, we return false.

187
00:07:02.357 --> 00:07:04.461
Otherwise the smaller tree hangs

188
00:07:04.527 --> 00:07:05.507
under the bigger one

189
00:07:05.507 --> 00:07:07.487
and the size is updated.

190
00:07:07.487 --> 00:07:08.557
Return true.

191
00:07:09.137 --> 00:07:11.455
Kruskal sorts a copy of the edge

192
00:07:11.527 --> 00:07:14.007
list with a comparator: by weight

193
00:07:14.007 --> 00:07:15.777
then by the two vertices.

194
00:07:16.127 --> 00:07:18.637
Then each edge goes through union

195
00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:21.007
and the log records every decision.

196
00:07:21.587 --> 00:07:24.309
Prim keeps candidate edges in a priority

197
00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:26.507
queue with the same comparator.

198
00:07:26.857 --> 00:07:28.407
We poll the cheapest.

199
00:07:28.407 --> 00:07:31.387
If its far end is in the tree, skip it.

200
00:07:31.387 --> 00:07:32.807
Otherwise add it

201
00:07:32.807 --> 00:07:35.617
and add the edges around the new vertex.

202
00:07:36.197 --> 00:07:37.647
The real run.

203
00:07:37.647 --> 00:07:39.927
Same accepts and rejects as in Python

204
00:07:39.927 --> 00:07:41.957
and the total is thirty-five.

205
00:07:42.307 --> 00:07:48.357
Prim adds D, F, B, E, C, G

206
00:07:48.357 --> 00:07:50.347
and the total is thirty-five as well.

207
00:07:50.927 --> 00:07:52.357
Same depths

208
00:07:52.357 --> 00:07:54.757
ten for a thousand twenty-four singletons

209
00:07:54.757 --> 00:07:57.007
and nine then one for the chain.

210
00:07:57.587 --> 00:08:00.337
Java has no union-find either.

211
00:08:00.337 --> 00:08:02.317
Sorting a list with a comparator

212
00:08:02.317 --> 00:08:04.097
and the priority queue class

213
00:08:04.097 --> 00:08:05.787
are the standard tools.

214
00:08:06.367 --> 00:08:08.617
Three mistakes to avoid.

215
00:08:08.617 --> 00:08:11.157
First, Kruskal without the cycle test

216
00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:13.967
it would simply add every edge.

217
00:08:14.317 --> 00:08:17.255
Second, Prim without skipping edges whose

218
00:08:17.327 --> 00:08:19.627
far end is already in the tree

219
00:08:19.627 --> 00:08:22.277
stale edges would add vertices twice.

220
00:08:22.627 --> 00:08:25.620
Third, mixing up the two kinds of tree.

221
00:08:25.697 --> 00:08:28.276
The minimum spanning tree is not

222
00:08:28.357 --> 00:08:31.147
the tree of cheapest routes.

223
00:08:31.727 --> 00:08:35.097
So in Java: union-find with compression

224
00:08:35.097 --> 00:08:37.197
sorted edges for Kruskal

225
00:08:37.197 --> 00:08:39.127
a priority queue for Prim.

226
00:08:39.477 --> 00:08:41.877
And that completes the graph chapter.

